{"chunk_id": "199da20af167", "text": "This section explains a divine chain of curses that sets up the birth of key figures in the Mahabharata. King Mahabhisha, a righteous ruler who reached heaven through great sacrifices, gazes improperly at the river\u2011goddess Ganga when her garments are blown aside in Brahma\u2019s court. For this lapse he is cursed to be reborn on earth, and Ganga is destined to cause him suffering there. Meanwhile the celestial Vasus are cursed by the sage Vasishtha to be born as humans after they unknowingly offend him. They beg Ganga to become their mother on earth and release them quickly by casting them into the river after birth. She agrees, but asks that one child survive. That surviving son\u2014destined to be childless\u2014will later be the great Bhishma, tying the cosmic curses to the origins of the Kuru dynasty.\nKing Mahabhisha is cursed after staring at Ganga in heaven; the Vasus, cursed by Vasishtha, arrange with Ganga to be born as her sons on earth.\nThemes: divine curses, fate and rebirth, Ganga and the Vasus, cosmic justice, origins of Santanu", "vector": [0.012793458998203278, -0.01802315004169941, -0.0027721417136490345, -0.003693933831527829, -0.021027008071541786, 0.022542467340826988, -0.02227184921503067, 0.01849673129618168, -0.013530893251299858, -0.02377377822995186, 0.009160414338111877, -0.009938441216945648, 0.024152643978595734, 0.02572222799062729, -0.012942299246788025, 0.013524127192795277, 0.04719575494527817, -0.022082416340708733, 0.03902309387922287, -0.011548616923391819, -0.0006727390573360026, -0.0002410190354567021, 0.0035518594086170197, 0.005919765681028366, -0.0335024893283844, 0.03485557809472084, 0.02974090166389942, -0.025654572993516922, -0.004658009856939316, -0.015059883706271648, -0.025789881125092506, 0.006230975966900587, -0.00920777302235365, -0.0024474002420902252, -0.018916187807917595, 0.011244172230362892, 0.003917193505913019, -0.013612078502774239, 0.00393072422593832, 0.0024896843